1807 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (              simple reactive modeling, the future of intelligent traffic systems lies in Mechanism Design \(often called "Reverse Game Theory"\). While our current project focuses on how agents adapt to a fixed environment, Mechanism Design allows the system architect to "engineer" the rules to ensure social efficiency.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1808 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Key strategic extensions include:)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1809 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Dynamic Congestion Pricing: Instead of static payoffs, the system can dynamically adjust "costs" \(tolls or time penalties\) for certain routes. This ensures that even self-interested agents choose paths that maximize the Social Welfare Function.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1810 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Incentive Compatibility: By using protocols like VCG \(Vickrey-Clarke-Groves\), we can design intersection priorities where agents are incentivized to report their true urgency, preventing system manipulation.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1811 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Pareto-Optimal Distribution: Shifting the system equilibrium from a simple Nash Equilibrium to a Pareto-optimal state where traffic is distributed such that no individual can improve their travel time without significantly harming the overall flow.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1812 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 88 0 R
/T (Shifting from reactive to proactive control. Mechanism Design engineers incentives to align individual driver goals with global traffic efficienc)
